block-pressure-ask = On which face will the block press on the table hardest?
block-pressure-title = What pressure does a block put on the table?
block-pressure-question = We weigh a wooden block on a dynamometer and measure its length, width and height with a ruler. The block can lie on the table on three different faces. On which face will it press on the table hardest?
block-pressure-plan-label = Wooden block
block-pressure-choices = On the largest face
block-pressure-choices-2 = On the smallest face
block-pressure-choices-3 = The same — the weight is the same
block-pressure-answer-why = The block presses on the table with the same force however it lies — its weight. But on a smaller face that force falls on a smaller area, and each square centimetre of the table gets a bigger share of it: p = F/S. That is why a sharp knife cuts (a small area — a large pressure) and wide skis do not sink into snow (a large area — a small pressure).
block-pressure-answer-say = Hardest on the smallest face!
block-pressure-answer-text = The block weighs P = { $value }, its edges are { $a } × { $b } × { $c }. The force on any face is the same — the weight, but the areas differ: { $faces }, { $faces2 } and { $faces3 } cm². So the pressure p = P/S differs too: ≈ { $p }, { $p2 } and { $p3 } Pa. The smaller the area, the larger the pressure — on the smallest face it is { $p4 } that on the largest. That is why knives are sharpened, and skis and caterpillar tracks are made wide.
